For a content-heavy site, you want your pages and images discoverable on as many channels as possible: Google (including image search), RSS readers, and Pinterest.
On Belle AI, I generate all three from the same typed content data using plain Next.js route handlers. No plugins needed.
Disclosure: I'm the developer of Belle AI.
My content lives in typed TypeScript modules: season palettes, lookbooks, face shape guides, blog posts. Each has slugs, titles, and image paths.
import { SEASON_PALETTES, SEASON_SLUGS } from "@/lib/color-palette-data";
import { LOOKBOOK_DATA } from "@/lib/lookbook-data";
import { getAllPosts } from "@/lib/blog";

The sitemap and the feeds all read from these modules. If I add a new lookbook entry, it appears in every output automatically. No second list to forget to update.
A file at app/sitemap-images.xml/route.ts is served at /sitemap-images.xml. Return a Response with the right content type:
export async function GET() {
  const entries: ImageEntry[] = buildEntries();
  const xml = `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9"
        xmlns:image="http://www.google.com/schemas/sitemap-image/1.1">
${entries.map(renderUrl).join("\n")}
</urlset>`;

  return new Response(xml, {
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": "application/xml; charset=utf-8",
      "Cache-Control": "public, s-maxage=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400",
    },
  });
}

Titles and captions contain &, quotes and apostrophes. One unescaped character makes the whole file invalid, and crawlers will silently drop it.
function esc(value: string): string {
  return value
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&apos;");
}

Escape & first, or you'll double-escape the others.
Sitemaps need absolute URLs. With a default locale that has no prefix and other locales that do, I use two small helpers:
function localePath(locale: string, path: string): string {
  const isDefault = locale === routing.defaultLocale;
  return isDefault
    ? `${SITE_URL}${path || "/"}`
    : `${SITE_URL}/${locale}${path}`;
}

function absUrl(imagePath: string): string {
  if (imagePath.startsWith("http")) return imagePath;
  return `${SITE_URL}${imagePath.startsWith("/") ? imagePath : `/${imagePath}`}`;
}

Use one SITE_URL constant everywhere. Mixing www and non-www hosts in a sitemap is a quiet way to create duplicate-content signals.
Each <url> can contain <image:image> entries, which helps Google discover images that are rendered by components or loaded lazily:
interface ImageEntry {
  loc: string;
  images: { url: string; title?: string; caption?: string }[];
}

For pages without a hand-picked image, I point to the dynamic opengraph-image route, so every page still contributes an image.
Pinterest can ingest an RSS feed, and each item needs an image. I reuse the same data and shape each item like this:
interface FeedItem {
  title: string;
  link: string;
  description: string;
  imageUrl: string;
  pubDate: string;
  category: string;
}

const items: FeedItem[] = [];

for (const post of getAllPosts("en")) {
  if (!post.image) continue; // Pinterest needs an image
  items.push({
    title: post.title,
    link: `${SITE_URL}/blog/${post.slug}`,
    description: post.description,
    imageUrl: absUrl(post.image),
    pubDate: new Date(post.date).toUTCString(),
    category: "Blog",
  });
}

The same loop pattern then adds lookbook images, transformation galleries and guides, each with its own category.
Because Next.js page alternates replace layout-level ones, I expose a feed per locale and advertise it from the same helper that builds canonical and hreflang tags:
function rssTypes(locale: string) {
  return {
    "application/rss+xml":
      locale === routing.defaultLocale
        ? `${SITE_URL}/feed.xml`
        : `${SITE_URL}/feed/blog/${locale}.xml`,
  };
}

Every page's <head> now points readers to the right feed for their language.
One typed data source feeds the sitemap, RSS and Pinterest feed
Escape all dynamic text in XML
Use absolute URLs and one canonical host
Skip items with no image in the Pinterest feed
Add cache headers so crawlers don't hammer your server
Advertise feeds in <head> via alternates.types



You can see the site these routes belong to here.
Do you generate feeds and sitemaps from content data like this, or do you use a plugin? Curious what trade-offs others have hit.

