Open your GitHub profile and look at it the way a recruiter would. There's a grid of green squares, a few pinned repositories and a language bar. You can read that page because you know what a contribution is. Someone who hires developers but doesn't write code can tell you were busy, and not much else.
gitreplay.club is meant for that moment. It turns your GitHub activity into a vertical, 20-second video you can send before an interview: commits, pull requests, top languages, your longest streak and a heatmap of the year. It's the same idea as the "GitHub Wrapped" recaps that go around every December, and you can make one any time of year, for any year back to 2008 or for a single month.
Rendering the video was the easy part. It's a Remotion composition rendered on AWS Lambda, and it takes about a minute and a half. Getting the numbers right was harder.
The obvious source for a commit count is contributionsCollection.totalCommitContributions in GitHub's GraphQL API. For a lot of developers that number is too low. It counts commits in public repositories. Commits in private repositories are pooled into restrictedContributionsCount, a single number that also includes other kinds of contributions and never says which repository they came from. If most of your year happened in private repos, as it does for many people who code for a living, the obvious number leaves most of your year out.
So gitreplay.club asks each repository instead. For every repo the token can read, it requests the default branch's history, filtered by author and date range:
defaultBranchRef {
  target {
    ... on Commit {
      history(author: { id: $authorId }, since: $since, until: $until) {
        totalCount
      }
    }
  }
}

The commit total is the sum of those counts, merge commits included, with GitHub's own figure kept as a floor. Some repositories stay unreadable even with your token, such as organization repos behind SAML single sign-on or OAuth app restrictions. Their commits come from GitHub's per-repository contribution data instead. Those repos don't trigger the video's partial-data warning, which only appears when a public repository has disappeared.
Languages took three tries. The first version added up the language bytes of every repository you own, the same data that draws the language bar on a repo page. That describes the history of your account rather than your year: a repository you last touched in 2019 counted as much as the one you worked in every day.
The second version tried to be exact. It walked every commit you authored in the period and ran the changed files through linguist-js, a port of GitHub's Linguist, to classify them. It was accurate, and it couldn't finish for prolific accounts. With one API request per commit, the cost grew with how much you wrote, and heavy users ran past the collection time budget and GitHub's secondary limit of 900 points per minute. It failed for exactly the people with the busiest years.
The version running now takes each repository's language mix and weights it by the commits you made there in the period. If a repo is 80% TypeScript and you made 200 commits there, that adds 160 commits' worth of TypeScript. It costs one GraphQL request per 25 repositories, however many commits you made. It doesn't measure which lines you personally wrote, and I'd rather say so here than let the chart imply it.
Then there's work GitHub can't see at all, like code on GitLab or in a company repo you lost access to when you changed jobs. Premium users can correct their numbers on a review screen before the video renders. Before a correction saves, you confirm the numbers are yours to report, and anyone watching the video or opening the share page sees a "Self-reported stats" label. A recap you could edit freely would be a résumé with animations, and a recruiter would be right to ignore it. One that can't be corrected is wrong for anyone whose work lives outside GitHub. The label is the compromise.
You can try it without signing in. Put any GitHub username after gitreplay.club/ and you get that account's public year, drawn as a terminal screen with the avatar in ASCII art, a bit like neofetch. Mine is gitreplay.club/fernandohre. That page only uses public data.
Signing in with GitHub adds your private work to the totals and lets you make the video. You review the numbers, pick a year or a month and a style, and the MP4 is ready in about a minute and a half. You can download it or share a link, and the share page can hide your repository names if your repos are client work. The video is free in the GitHub Dark style, with a watermark. Premium is a one-time US$15 that adds four more styles, removes the watermark and turns on the self-reported corrections.
If a number in your recap looks wrong, tell me which one in the comments and what you expected it to be.

AI disclosure: I used AI assistance to draft and edit this article. I reviewed the technical claims against the browser documentation linked below and checked the examples for consistency. The code is intentionally simplified.
An AI assistant can produce a polished HTML demo in a minute. The page has buttons, calculations, a form, and maybe a chart. It looks like an application, so it is easy to assume that publishing the HTML also publishes everything behind it.
That last step needs a closer look. A static page can render, calculate, filter, animate, and remember a preference in one browser. It cannot safely hold a private API key, turn every visitor's browser into your shared database, or make a service running only on your laptop available to everyone else.
Here is the checklist I use to decide whether a generated page is ready for static hosting or needs a backend.
This code can make a feedback form look finished:
const items = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('feedback') || '[]');
items.push({ message, createdAt: new Date().toISOString() });
localStorage.setItem('feedback', JSON.stringify(items));
render(items);

It preserves the current visitor's entries in that browser. It does not send them to the page owner, and another visitor will not see them. Browser storage is useful for a draft, a preference, or a local-only tool. It is not a shared submissions database.
There is another preview trap: the required behavior of localStorage for file: URLs is undefined and can vary between browsers. A result you see after double-clicking an HTML file is not a reliable test of what storage will do after deployment.
If you only need a visual demonstration, keep the data in memory or use a small fixture and label the action clearly: "Demo only — nothing is submitted." If someone must receive the submission, use an HTTPS endpoint and show what happened after the server responds.
localhost means


Generated code often contains a call like this:
const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/quote');
const quote = await response.json();

While you develop, that address can reach a server on your machine. After you send the page to somebody else, the same code runs in their browser. localhost then means their device, not yours.
A relative URL does not magically preserve your development backend either:
fetch('/api/quote');

On a static host, this normally asks that host for /api/quote. If no such endpoint exists, the page may receive a 404 or an HTML fallback instead of JSON.
For a self-contained demo, replace the request with explicit sample data and keep the simulated behavior visible. For live data, deploy an endpoint your visitors can reach, then test its response and CORS policy from the public page.
A private credential does not become safe because it sits inside a JavaScript variable or an HTML comment:
// Simplified anti-example: never publish a real privileged credential here.
const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/private-data', {
  headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer SERVER_ONLY_KEY' }
});

Every visitor can download the page and inspect its scripts. Obfuscation changes how the credential looks; it does not establish a secret boundary.
Before publishing, inspect the actual export directory rather than only the project root. A bundler can copy a secret into its output even when .env itself is excluded. Check inline scripts, source maps, generated configuration, fixtures, and comments too. Remove private customer data at the same time.
If a privileged API call is required, keep that credential on a server. The browser calls your limited endpoint; your endpoint validates the request and calls the upstream service:
// Server-side route sketch. Add authentication, validation, and rate limits for a real service.
export async function GET() {
  const upstream = await fetch('https://api.example.com/private-data', {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.SERVICE_API_KEY}` }
  });

  if (!upstream.ok) {
    // Do not pass an upstream secret or sensitive response through by default.
    return Response.json({ error: 'Upstream request failed' }, { status: 502 });
  }

  return Response.json(await upstream.json());
}

Some client-side API identifiers are deliberately public and can be restricted by origin or scope. That is a provider-specific design, so follow the provider's documentation. Do not treat a server credential as public just because the variable name contains KEY.
A local file, a development server, and a public HTTPS page are different environments.
JavaScript modules loaded from file: URLs can trigger CORS errors. Test modules through an HTTP server, and make sure the server returns JavaScript files with a JavaScript-compatible MIME type. An apparently missing module can be a delivery problem rather than an import mistake.
For a public page that calls another origin, that server's CORS policy controls whether browser JavaScript may read the response. mode: 'no-cors' is not a general way to unlock JSON for your application. If you control the API, configure the origins it should serve; if you do not, a server-side integration may be the right boundary.
Also check for http:// dependencies in an HTTPS page. Browsers restrict mixed content, and fetch() requests to insecure resources can fail. Camera, microphone, service worker, and modern clipboard features have additional secure-context and permission requirements. http://localhost receives special treatment for development, which can hide a deployment problem.
Feature detection is more useful than assuming the preview and the public page have the same capabilities:
if (window.isSecureContext && navigator.clipboard?.writeText) {
  await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
} else {
  // Show selectable text and a clear manual-copy instruction.
  showManualCopy(text);
}

A static page is a good fit when the result depends on the current visitor's input and public assets: a calculator, a proposal preview, a chart from bundled data, or a small editing tool whose output can be downloaded.
Add a backend when the product needs shared state or authority: submissions that another person must read, accounts and permissions, shared edits, private upstream credentials, privileged actions, or durable data across visitors and devices.
Before sharing the page, run this short review:
Open the public URL while logged out, ideally on a second device. Test one important interaction and a narrow screen.
Inspect the Network panel during that interaction. Check the final URL, status, response type, CORS errors, and mixed-content errors.
Search the delivered files for private data and credentials. Do not publish the whole source directory when only the built output is meant to be public.
Submit the form and explain where the data went. Distinguish a local simulation from a server-confirmed submission.
Check clipboard, camera, microphone, and offline behavior under the real deployment protocol. Provide a usable fallback when a capability is unavailable.
The goal is not to add a backend to every generated page. It is to make the page honest about where computation, data, and authority live. Once those boundaries are clear, static hosting can stay simple without pretending to provide server behavior.
