For healthtech backend task monitoring, choose tools that can answer the failure in front of you: an import watchdog catches “the task ran, but no usable result arrived,” while health check APIs and synthetic checks answer “can this endpoint respond right now?” They are complementary, but they do not produce the same evidence or belong on the same alert.



Approach
Evidence it produces
Pick it when
Main boundary




Import watchdog
A scheduled run produced a count, timestamp, and outcome
Missing or stale business data is the incident
It cannot prove that a user-facing API is reachable


Synthetic health check
A request crossed a network boundary and received an expected response
Availability of a route or dependency is the incident
A healthy response can hide a stalled importer


Pull-based metrics and rules
Time series showing freshness and error state
The team needs history, aggregation, and alert routing
It needs an exporter or collector to observe the job



The least complex design is a small Node.js watchdog that records one outcome per import, plus a health endpoint that reports process and dependency readiness. The watchdog owns freshness. The endpoint owns reachability. Cost attribution becomes much clearer when each alert has one observable claim.
Start with the sentence an on-call engineer should be able to complete: “We are paging because ___.” For a scheduled import, useful completions include “the 06:00 run has not produced a result,” “the source returned zero records three times,” or “the importer is failing validation.” “The service is up” is a different sentence.
The trap is treating a successful HTTP response as proof that the backend task succeeded.
Keep the claims separate.
That distinction matters in healthcare workflows. A process can return HTTP 200 while it has an empty payload, an old cursor, or a parser that discarded every row. A process can also be healthy while its public route is unreachable from outside the cluster. Treating those cases as one binary status creates noisy incidents and hides the cost of the check that found them.
I use two timestamps in the import record: startedAt and completedAt. I also keep recordsAccepted, sourceBatchId, and a normalized outcome. The timestamp used for freshness is the completion time, not the scheduler's planned time. That small choice avoids paging on a slow run that still delivered valid data.
Here is the boundary in words: the scheduler emits an event; the importer turns the event into an outcome; the watchdog measures the age of the latest successful outcome; the health endpoint reports whether the process can serve requests. An alerting rule consumes one of those signals and names its owner.
The health route should be cheap and bounded. It can check that the process is accepting requests and that required configuration is present. A readiness route may test a database connection with a short timeout. It should not start an import, scan a whole result table, or wait for a third-party source. Those operations turn a probe into an accidental workload. The API proves reachability; the task record proves freshness.
The watchdog can carry the slower, domain-specific check. This TypeScript sketch keeps the external interface generic and makes the decision visible in the data rather than hiding it in a vendor-specific callback:
type ImportOutcome = {
  startedAt: string;
  completedAt: string;
  recordsAccepted: number;
  sourceBatchId: string;
  outcome: "success" | "empty" | "failed";
};

type ImportStore = {
  latestSuccessful(): Promise<ImportOutcome | null>;
  save(outcome: ImportOutcome): Promise<void>;
};

export async function runImport(store: ImportStore): Promise<ImportOutcome> {
  const startedAt = new Date().toISOString();

  try {
    const batch = await fetchSourceBatch();
    const accepted = validateAndCount(batch.records);
    const outcome: ImportOutcome = {
      startedAt,
      completedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      recordsAccepted: accepted,
      sourceBatchId: batch.id,
      outcome: accepted === 0 ? "empty" : "success",
    };

    await store.save(outcome);
    return outcome;
  } catch (error) {
    const outcome: ImportOutcome = {
      startedAt,
      completedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      recordsAccepted: 0,
      sourceBatchId: "unknown",
      outcome: "failed",
    };

    await store.save(outcome);
    throw error;
  }
}

declare function fetchSourceBatch(): Promise<{
  id: string;
  records: unknown[];
}>;

declare function validateAndCount(records: unknown[]): number;

The empty outcome is deliberate. It is not automatically an incident: some source systems legitimately publish no records. The policy should decide whether one empty batch is informational and whether repeated empties, measured over a defined window, are actionable. That policy belongs beside the data contract, where a reviewer can change it without rewriting probe code.
For the freshness calculation, keep the threshold in the same time unit as the schedule. If imports are expected every 30 minutes, a rule might mark the stream stale after 75 minutes. The extra 45 minutes is a policy choice for source delay, retries, and deployment pauses; it is not a universal magic number. Record the reason for the margin.
Cost attribution is easier when checks are assigned to work they can explain. A watchdog run has an execution cost and a data-processing cost. A synthetic check has request, network, and probe-frequency costs. Pull-based metrics add collection and retention costs. None of these should be disguised as a single “monitoring” line item.
That is a real trade-off. A backend tool that records every task outcome gives better failure evidence, but it adds persistence and retention work. A health-check API is lighter, but it cannot expose stale business data. Pick the cheaper evidence only when it still proves the incident you care about.
The product landscape reflects these shapes, without making the products interchangeable. Healthchecks.io is built around reporting scheduled-job completion through a ping. Prometheus is built around time-series collection and rule evaluation. Grafana Cloud includes metrics and synthetic monitoring as separate observability capabilities. Better Stack documents uptime checks alongside incident workflows. These are useful examples of different boundaries; the right comparison is the signal and its retention, not a feature-count contest.
A practical allocation table might assign the importer to the data platform budget, external route probes to platform reliability, and long-lived metric retention to shared observability. The labels matter more than the dashboard. They let a team ask why a check runs, who acts on it, and how long its evidence must remain available.
The useful review is a short cost-and-evidence exercise, not a hunt for the longest feature list. Draw one line for the scheduled task and one for the public API. On the task line, record the run frequency, the outcome record, the freshness window, and the retention period needed for investigation. On the API line, record the probe interval, the network locations that matter, the response timeout, and the amount of history needed to see an availability pattern. If a proposed tool adds another signal, write down the decision that signal changes. If nobody can name that decision, the signal is overhead. If the signal replaces a manual check during a data incident, it may be worth the operational cost even when it does not reduce compute usage. This is the part that prevents a cheap-looking health endpoint from becoming an expensive source of false confidence.
OpenTelemetry helps keep the vocabulary portable: logs describe discrete events, while metrics describe measurements over time. Emit the import outcome as a structured event and expose freshness as a measurement. Do not force a high-cardinality batch identifier into a metric label. Put it in the event or trace context instead. That preserves queryability without turning every source batch into a new time series.
Write the failure cases before choosing a monitoring service. For each case, specify the observed signal, the owner, the delay allowed, and the action. Then test the importer with a valid batch, an empty batch, malformed records, a timeout, and a retry that eventually succeeds. Health routes need their own tests for a ready process, a missing dependency, and a timeout.
The probe response should be stable enough for automation, but useful enough for an engineer. This is a deliberately small example:
type Readiness = {
  status: "ready" | "not_ready";
  checks: Record<string, "pass" | "fail">;
};

export async function readiness(
  checkDatabase: () => Promise<boolean>,
): Promise<Readiness> {
  const database = await Promise.race([
    checkDatabase(),
    new Promise<boolean>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(false), 800)),
  ]);

  return {
    status: database ? "ready" : "not_ready",
    checks: { database: database ? "pass" : "fail" },
  };
}

Do not let a failed readiness check erase the last successful import timestamp. Those are separate facts. Alert routing can then distinguish “the API cannot accept traffic” from “fresh data is missing,” and a post-incident review can identify which check created the page.
That separation pays off during review.
For a real deployment, also define deduplication and recovery behavior. One alert should recover when the same condition clears, not every time a probe happens to pass. Keep a small sample of the raw event around long enough to answer what the importer received and accepted. That evidence is often more valuable than another dashboard panel.
An import watchdog cannot tell you that a clinician can reach the API. A synthetic health check cannot tell you that the last successful import contained complete, current data. Metrics can show a trend, but they cannot replace the payload-level evidence needed to investigate a rejected batch. A lightweight health check is a poor fit for a data-freshness incident; a full watchdog is unnecessary for a route-only availability question.
There is also a human limit. Every distinct failure claim needs an owner and a runbook. If the team cannot say what a notification means or what action follows, increasing probe frequency will only increase interruptions. Start with the smallest pair of signals that separates freshness from reachability, then add detail when an observed failure proves the need.
