I have watched the same failure play out in real-time features more than once. One server, one room, everything works. You add a second instance behind a load balancer to handle more connections, and half your users stop receiving messages. Nobody changed the room code. Nobody changed the gateway. The system just got bigger, and the thing that worked at one instance quietly stopped working at two.
The cause is simple, and that is what makes it dangerous. Socket.io rooms live in memory by default. Instance A knows its own sockets. Instance B knows its own sockets. Neither knows the other's. When instance A broadcasts to a room, it only talks to the sockets it can see.
This post is the fix I reach for in NestJS: the Redis adapter, the sticky-session rule that makes it actually work, and a room pattern that stops you from rebuilding the same in-memory trap in a new place. It is written for a NestJS app that already has a working gateway on one instance and now needs a second one.
A single Socket.io server holds every room, every socket, and every membership in its own process memory, so a broadcast to a room is a local loop over local sockets. Add a second instance and that loop still only covers local sockets, which means a message sent from instance A never reaches a room member connected to instance B. Nothing errors, no exception is thrown, and the sender's own test client often passes because it happens to be connected to the sending instance.
That silent half-delivery is the signature of this bug. If your chat, live feed, or collaboration room works for some users and not others, and the split lines up with which instance they landed on, you are almost certainly looking at in-memory rooms behind a load balancer rather than a bug in your room logic.
The Redis adapter replaces the in-memory room store with a shared channel that every instance listens to, so a broadcast becomes a publish step plus a local delivery step on each instance. When instance A emits to a room, it publishes that event to Redis; instance B receives it through its subscription and delivers it to its own local sockets in that room, and instance A does the same for its locals. Your gateway code does not change shape: server.to(room).emit(...) keeps working, and the adapter changes where that call reaches.
Two details in that picture matter for everything that follows. First, the adapter moves messages between servers, but it does not move your application state between servers. A map you built in instance A's memory is still invisible to instance B. Second, Redis becomes part of your real-time path, so a Redis outage is now a real-time outage unless you have planned for it. I cover both failure modes below, because the adapter solves the broadcast problem and creates two smaller problems you need to handle on purpose.
Start from the documented NestJS shape and do not improvise the wiring. Install the three packages, extend IoAdapter, connect two Redis clients, and register the adapter before the app starts listening. The two-client split is not optional decoration: a Redis connection in subscriber mode cannot issue regular commands, so the publishing client and the subscribing client must be separate connections, and duplicate() gives the subscriber the same configuration on its own connection.
npm install redis socket.io @socket.io/redis-adapter

// redis-io.adapter.ts
import { IoAdapter } from '@nestjs/platform-socket.io';
import { ServerOptions } from 'socket.io';
import { createAdapter } from '@socket.io/redis-adapter';
import { createClient } from 'redis';

export class RedisIoAdapter extends IoAdapter {
  private adapterConstructor: ReturnType<typeof createAdapter>;

  async connectToRedis(): Promise<void> {
    const pubClient = createClient({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL });
    const subClient = pubClient.duplicate();

    await Promise.all([pubClient.connect(), subClient.connect()]);
    this.adapterConstructor = createAdapter(pubClient, subClient);
  }

  createIOServer(port: number, options?: ServerOptions): any {
    const server = super.createIOServer(port, options);
    server.adapter(this.adapterConstructor);
    return server;
  }
}

// main.ts
import { NestFactory } from '@nestjs/core';
import { AppModule } from './app.module';
import { RedisIoAdapter } from './redis-io.adapter';

async function bootstrap() {
  const app = await NestFactory.create(AppModule);
  const redisIoAdapter = new RedisIoAdapter(app);
  await redisIoAdapter.connectToRedis();
  app.useWebSocketAdapter(redisIoAdapter);
  await app.listen(3000);
}
bootstrap();

Three things in that code deserve a second look before you ship it. Connect both clients before the app listens, or early broadcasts can run before the adapter is ready. Read the Redis URL from the environment, never hardcode localhost, or your staging and production setups will diverge in exactly the way that wastes a debugging afternoon. And keep the adapter construction in one place: every gateway in the app shares this adapter through useWebSocketAdapter, so a second copy of the wiring in another module is a second chance to drift. This is the same production NestJS stack I describe on my hiring page, where Socket.io and BullMQ on Redis are the real-time and background pair I ship with.
The Redis adapter moves broadcasts between instances, but it does not fix the handshake, and this is the step that sinks most first attempts at scaling Socket.io. A Socket.io client starts with HTTP long polling and then upgrades to a WebSocket connection, and those polling requests must reach the same instance that owns the session. If your load balancer round-robins a polling request to a different instance, that instance does not know the session, and the client gets errors and reconnect loops instead of a working socket.
The NestJS docs state the rule plainly: with multiple load-balanced instances you must either disable polling by setting transports: ['websocket'] in the client configuration, or enable cookie-based routing (sticky sessions) in your load balancer, because Redis alone is not enough. Pick one deliberately:



Approach
What it costs you
When I pick it




WebSocket-only transport
You lose the polling fallback, so clients behind strict proxies that block WebSocket upgrades cannot connect at all
Internal tools, controlled clients, or a first scaled deployment where you can measure how many clients actually need polling


Cookie-based sticky sessions
Your load balancer must support it and you must test it, since a misconfigured cookie rule fails exactly like no rule at all
Any public-facing app, because it keeps the polling fallback working while still pinning a session to one instance



Do not substitute IP-based hashing and call it done without thinking. Users behind corporate NAT, mobile networks, and shared Wi-Fi can share an IP or change it mid-session, and IP hashing pins them by an address that is not really theirs. A cookie set by the load balancer tracks the session, not the network path, and that is the property you actually need. Whichever option you pick, test it with polling enabled and with two instances running, because a sticky rule that only works in a single-instance test is not a sticky rule.
Once broadcasts cross instances, the next trap is addressing. The question stops being "how do I emit to a room" and becomes "how do I reach this one user, or this one tenant's room, when I have no idea which instance holds their socket". The answer is to stop asking which instance holds them. Address the room, and let the adapter find the instance.
The pattern I use has two rules. First, every socket joins a room named for its user immediately after authentication, before it joins anything else. Second, every shared room is named for the thing it belongs to, not for a server or a process, so a tenant room looks like tenant:9:project:41 and a user room looks like user:42. A service that needs to notify one user then emits to user:42 from any instance, and the adapter delivers it to whichever instance holds that socket.
// rooms.gateway.ts
import {
  WebSocketGateway,
  WebSocketServer,
  SubscribeMessage,
  OnGatewayConnection,
  MessageBody,
  ConnectedSocket,
} from '@nestjs/websockets';
import { Server, Socket } from 'socket.io';

@WebSocketGateway({ cors: { origin: process.env.CLIENT_ORIGIN } })
export class RoomsGateway implements OnGatewayConnection {
  @WebSocketServer()
  server: Server;

  async handleConnection(client: Socket) {
    // Authenticate first. Only join rooms for a verified user id.
    const userId = client.data.userId as string;
    await client.join(`user:${userId}`);
  }

  @SubscribeMessage('join-project')
  async onJoinProject(
    @MessageBody() projectId: string,
    @ConnectedSocket() client: Socket,
  ) {
    // Check membership in your database here, then join.
    await client.join(`project:${projectId}`);
    return { joined: projectId };
  }

  async notifyProject(projectId: string, payload: unknown) {
    // Callable from any instance. The adapter routes it.
    this.server.to(`project:${projectId}`).emit('project-updated', payload);
  }
}

That gateway sketch leaves two things deliberately out, and both are worth naming. Authentication is shown as a placeholder because token validation belongs in your own guard or handshake check, not in a copy-pasted snippet, and a socket that joins a room before authentication is a socket that can listen to a room it should never see. Membership checks are also a placeholder: joining project:41 must be gated on a database check that this user belongs to project 41, because a room name is not a permission. Validate the join payload before trusting it too; I use Zod at every boundary, as I wrote up in Zod as a contract. The adapter will faithfully deliver messages to anyone in the room, so the join step is where your access control lives.
One more habit pays off here. When you need to know who is actually connected, use the adapter's own tools, such as fetchSockets, instead of a local map. A local map answers "who is connected to this instance". The adapter answers "who is connected to this system", and at two or more instances only the second question is useful.
Socket.io now ships two Redis-backed adapters, and the choice between them is a real decision rather than a version detail. The classic adapter forwards packets through Redis pub/sub. The newer Redis Streams adapter forwards them through a Redis stream, and its docs give it one clear advantage: it handles a temporary disconnection from Redis and resumes the stream without losing packets, where the classic pub/sub adapter does not offer that resume behavior. Both still need sticky sessions, and the Streams docs say so directly, so the new adapter does not remove the handshake work from the previous section.



Adapter
How messages move
Disconnect behavior
NestJS wiring




Classic @socket.io/redis-adapter

Redis pub/sub, two clients
