My pick is boring on purpose: start with the classic adapter from the NestJS docs. It is the documented path, it matches the examples other NestJS developers will recognize when they read your code, and its failure mode is one you can plan around with reconnect handling and client-side resync. Move to the Streams adapter when temporary Redis disconnects are a real problem in your deployment, not a theoretical one, and when you have time to verify the custom wiring against your own gateway tests. A newer adapter is not automatically the right adapter for a system that is already working.
A scaling change that has never been observed across two instances is a guess, and this one is cheap to observe. Run Redis locally, start your NestJS app twice on two ports, connect one client to each instance, and then trigger a broadcast from a service running on only one of them. If both clients receive it, the adapter is working. If only the client on the sending instance receives it, check the adapter registration first, then the Redis connection, then the room name, in that order, because those three cover most wiring mistakes.
Make the test adversarial in one specific way: connect with polling enabled, not websocket-only, and watch what happens when the load balancer is in the path. A setup that passes with a direct connection to one instance and fails behind the balancer has a sticky-session problem, not an adapter problem, and no amount of adapter debugging will fix it. The same test with websocket-only transport passing and polling failing points at the same place. Name the failure before you fix it, or you will fix the wrong layer twice.
Also verify the negative case. Disconnect a client, then broadcast to its room, and confirm nothing crashes and no stale delivery appears when it reconnects. Room membership is tied to the socket, not the user, so a reconnected client must rejoin its rooms after authentication. If your client code assumes rooms survive a reconnect, your users will silently stop receiving messages after every network drop, and that failure looks exactly like the original scaling bug.
Putting Redis in the middle of your real-time path changes what an outage means, so plan for it before it happens. The classic adapter has no stream to resume from, so messages published while Redis is unreachable do not wait politely for it to come back. Your clients need a resync path for exactly this case: on reconnect, the client asks for current state over HTTP or replays its room joins, and the server answers with the latest state rather than assuming the client saw every event. That resync endpoint is not extra polish. It is the thing that turns a Redis blip into a brief catch-up instead of a permanently stale screen.
Three more failure modes deserve a line each. First, keep payloads small. Every broadcast is published to Redis and then fanned out to every instance, so a large payload is multiplied by your instance count before it reaches a single client. Send ids and small deltas, and let clients fetch full objects over HTTP when they need them. Second, treat Redis as trusted internal infrastructure. The Socket.io docs warn that adapter messages are not signed or encrypted by the adapter, so do not expose Redis to untrusted networks, use authentication and TLS where your setup supports them, and never put long-lived secrets in event payloads. Third, watch the fan-out itself: a room with thousands of members across many instances turns one emit into thousands of deliveries, and that is a product decision as much as an infrastructure one. If a room grows that large, split it by topic or shard it by tenant before you scale the instance count to compensate.



