If your NestJS app has one instance today and needs two tomorrow, I would pick the classic Redis adapter from the NestJS docs, cookie-based sticky sessions at the load balancer, and room-per-user addressing from day one. That combination is documented, it is easy for the next developer to reason about, and it fixes the actual failure: broadcasts that only reach local sockets.
I would not pick the Streams adapter first, even though its disconnect behavior is better, because the documented path matters more than the newer feature when you are changing a live real-time system. I would not pick websocket-only transport for a public app, because giving up the polling fallback trades one visible failure for a quieter one on restrictive networks. And I would not ship any of it without the two-instance test above, because a scaling setup you have not watched fail and recover is a setup you do not understand yet.
The answer flips in two cases. If you already measure packet loss during Redis disconnects and users notice stale screens after every blip, the Streams adapter earns its custom wiring. If your clients are all internal and controlled, websocket-only transport is simpler than sticky sessions and the tradeoff disappears. Outside those cases, the boring documented combination is the right default, and boring is a compliment when the system in question is the one your users stare at all day.
If you are building a multi-tenant real-time system and want a second pair of eyes on the room design before it ships, that is the kind of production NestJS work I do. Reach me through the contact section on the homepage with the room model, the instance count, and the failure you are seeing, and we can walk through it in a first call.
By default each Socket.io server keeps its rooms in its own memory. A broadcast from instance A only reaches sockets connected to A, so a user on instance B never sees it. The Redis adapter fixes this by forwarding broadcasts through Redis, so every instance delivers the message to its own local sockets in that room.
Yes. The NestJS docs warn about this directly: Redis alone is not enough. Socket.io starts with HTTP long polling and then upgrades to WebSocket, and the polling requests must reach the same instance that owns the session. Either force websocket-only transport or turn on cookie-based routing at your load balancer.
A Redis connection in subscriber mode cannot issue regular commands, so one connection cannot both publish and subscribe. The standard setup creates a publishing client and then duplicates it for subscribing. That split is in the NestJS docs example and it is the shape to copy, not a detail to simplify away.
Start with the classic Redis adapter if you are following the NestJS docs, because that is the documented NestJS path. Consider the Redis Streams adapter when temporary Redis disconnects matter to you, because it resumes the stream without losing packets where the classic pub/sub adapter does not. Either way you still need sticky sessions.
Put every socket in a room named for its user, such as user:42, right after authentication. Then any instance can emit to that room and the adapter routes it to the instance holding that socket. Never keep a socketId to userId map in one instance's memory and expect other instances to read it.
Usually one of three things: the load balancer sends polling requests to the wrong instance, one instance holds state the others cannot see, or a broadcast payload grows until Redis fan-out becomes the bottleneck. Fix them in that order: sticky sessions first, room-based addressing second, and smaller payloads plus fewer broadcast rooms third.

Executive Summary


Chat assistants that only draft text are giving way to agents that act: they browse sites, fill in forms, run code and complete multi-step tasks. Three of the best-known examples are OpenAI’s computer-using agent (launched as Operator and now part of ChatGPT agent), Cognition’s Devin, and Manus. Teams are trying them on revenue-adjacent work such as researching accounts, benchmarking vendors, filling procurement and billing forms, and checking contract terms.
Enterprise sales workflows are rarely linear. An agent will hit an unlisted pricing tier, a custom security questionnaire or a contract value above its pre-approved limit. At that point, unchecked autonomy becomes a liability.
This guide describes a human-in-the-loop (HITL) control plane for those moments. A policy layer pauses the agent before a sensitive action, captures context, and posts an interactive Slack message to a sales “war room” channel. An account executive (AE) or revenue engineer can claim the deal, approve it, modify it or reject it. The agent then resumes with that decision, or the human takes over the session.
A caveat up front: you can only intercept an agent at the points where you control its execution. The article covers where those points are for each of these products, and which parts of the pattern are vendor features and which you build yourself.
Operator, Devin and Manus have all changed since they launched. Check the current state of each before designing around it.
OpenAI Operator → ChatGPT agent. OpenAI released Operator on January 23, 2025, as a research preview for ChatGPT Pro users in the US. It was powered by a model called Computer-Using Agent (CUA), which combines GPT-4o’s vision with reinforcement-learning-based reasoning to interact with graphical interfaces. On July 17, 2025, OpenAI folded Operator into ChatGPT as ChatGPT agent, which also absorbs deep research. OpenAI said the standalone operator.chatgpt.com site would be deprecated, and OpenAI’s Help Center now says the Operator website is no longer accessible. ChatGPT agent launched for Pro, Plus and Team users, and Enterprise and Edu plans followed on August 8, 2025.
Devin (Cognition). Devin is marketed as an autonomous AI software engineer that works with a shell, code editor and browser. Cognition acquired Windsurf in July 2025, and the Windsurf editor was rebranded Devin Desktop on June 2, 2026. Pricing has also changed. Public trackers list Free, Pro ($20/month), Max ($200/month), Teams ($80/month plus $40 per full developer seat) and custom Enterprise tiers as of mid-2026. Verify current pricing at devin.ai/pricing.
Manus. Manus launched in March 2025 as a general-purpose agent platform that browses the web, writes reports and executes tasks on a user’s behalf. Meta announced in December 2025 that it would acquire it for a reported $2 billion or more. China’s National Development and Reform Commission ordered the deal unwound on April 27, 2026. In August 2026 Manus said it would resume operating as an independent company. In October 2026 its parent, Butterfly Effect, announced a funding round of more than $500 million. If you build on Manus, plan for account and data changes during the separation: Manus warned in August that data generated by some users on or after December 29, 2025 would be deleted for regulatory reasons.
Computer-using agents run a loop of perception, reasoning and action. OpenAI’s CUA, for example, receives screenshots of the screen, reasons about the next step, and then issues mouse and keyboard actions. This is why such agents can work on websites that have no API. It also means they interact with pixels and UI elements rather than parsing the page’s DOM directly, which affects how you intercept them (see Section 2).
Their suitability for sales work differs:
ChatGPT agent: Works in a virtual browser and can fill in forms, research accounts and produce files. OpenAI states it is designed to ask for the user’s input at critical points, and Operator hands control back to the user for tasks such as logins and CAPTCHAs. OpenAI also said Operator was limited from higher-risk tasks such as banking transactions.
Devin: Best suited to technical work, such as auditing repositories, estimating migration effort, or running a proof of concept for a technical buyer. It is not a purchasing or contract-negotiation agent, so treat claims that it “evaluates licensing tiers” with caution.
Manus: A general-purpose research and execution agent, useful for market research, competitive benchmarking and report generation.
Agents handle standard workflows well, but enterprise transactions hit friction points that need a human:
Pricing or budget threshold exceeded. A renewal or expansion crosses a pre-authorized limit and needs sign-off.
Vendor gatekeeping. A vendor site presents a custom-quote form, a login challenge or a CAPTCHA mid-flow.
Custom legal or security terms. A procurement form asks for non-standard Master Services Agreement (MSA) language or a SOC 2 Type II report.
Non-standard discounting or bundling. A request falls outside approved discount tiers.
Routing these moments to people through Slack lets the agent do the repetitive work while humans keep authority over the high-stakes decisions.
How you intercept depends on who runs the agent:
Agents you orchestrate yourself. If you build on OpenAI’s computer-use tooling in its API, or on another agent framework, your code executes every action the model proposes. You can place a policy check between “model proposes action” and “action executes.” This is the most reliable place for a hard gate. OpenAI’s API tooling also supports safety checks that require the developer to acknowledge them before continuing.
Hosted products (ChatGPT agent, Devin, Manus). These run in the vendor’s own sandboxed environment, so you cannot insert a proxy into their browser. You can still use their built-in pause or takeover prompts and approval settings, restrict what the agent can reach through connectors and credentials, and scope its permissions so it cannot reach a purchase or signature step on its own. Where a product exposes an API or webhooks, use those events to trigger the Slack flow.
The pattern in this guide is the first case: a middleware state broker that sits in your own agent loop. For hosted agents, apply the same policy logic to whatever hooks and permissions the vendor exposes.
