State interceptor. Wraps the agent’s action loop. It records the proposed action, the current step, the intent log and a screenshot before anything executes.
Policy and risk engine. Evaluates each proposed action against business rules such as maximum deal value, maximum discount, restricted regions, and a list of actions that always require approval (sending payment details, accepting terms, signing).
Slack messaging gateway. Turns the intercepted context into a Block Kit message with action buttons.
Session store and resume handlers. Persist the agent’s state (task, plan, memory buffer, browser session reference) while the agent waits, with a timeout so stale sessions do not linger. Never store credentials or payment data in the Slack payload.
The following Node.js/TypeScript example shows the pattern. It is a blueprint rather than production code: the session-store, pause and resume functions are placeholders for your own infrastructure. It uses Slack’s Bolt for JavaScript framework, which verifies Slack’s request signatures for you.
// agentInterceptor.ts
import { sendSlackWarRoomAlert } from './slackAlert';

export interface AgentState {
  agentId: string;
  agentRuntime: string;        // e.g. "custom-cua", "devin", "manus"
  targetCompany: string;
  proposedACV: number;         // annual contract value in USD
  currentStep: string;
  sessionId: string;
  screenshotUrl: string;       // must be a publicly reachable HTTPS URL (see 3.2)
}

// Illustrative policy: tune to your own approval matrix.
const APPROVAL_THRESHOLD_USD = 25_000;

export async function evaluateAgentAction(state: AgentState): Promise<'proceed' | 'halted'> {
  if (state.proposedACV >= APPROVAL_THRESHOLD_USD) {
    await pauseAgentSession(state.sessionId);
    await sendSlackWarRoomAlert(state);
    return 'halted';
  }
  return 'proceed';
}

async function pauseAgentSession(sessionId: string): Promise<void> {
  // Persist state and stop issuing actions for this session.
  // Add an expiry (for example 30 minutes) after which the session is
  // aborted or escalated, so paused sessions never hang indefinitely.
}

Slack’s image block requires a publicly accessible image_url, so upload screenshots to storage that Slack can reach (for example a short-lived signed URL). Screenshots may contain customer data, so apply your retention and access rules.
// slackAlert.ts
import { WebClient } from '@slack/web-api';
import type { AgentState } from './agentInterceptor';

const slack = new WebClient(process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN);
const WAR_ROOM_CHANNEL = process.env.SLACK_WAR_ROOM_CHANNEL_ID!;

export async function sendSlackWarRoomAlert(state: AgentState) {
  const deal = `$${state.proposedACV.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;

  await slack.chat.postMessage({
    channel: WAR_ROOM_CHANNEL,
    text: `High-value deal paused for review: ${state.targetCompany} (${deal})`, // notification fallback
    blocks: [
      {
        type: 'header',
        text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Agent paused: human review required', emoji: true },
      },
      {
        type: 'section',
        fields: [
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Account:*\n${state.targetCompany}` },
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Detected deal size:*\n${deal}` },
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Agent:*\n${state.agentRuntime}` },
          { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Current step:*\n${state.currentStep}` },
        ],
      },
      {
        type: 'image',
        title: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Browser state at pause' },
        image_url: state.screenshotUrl,
        alt_text: 'Screenshot of the agent browser at the moment it was paused',
      },
      {
        type: 'actions',
        block_id: `agent_actions_${state.sessionId}`,
        elements: [
          {
            type: 'button',
            text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Claim & take over', emoji: true },
            style: 'primary',
            value: state.sessionId,
            action_id: 'claim_deal_session',
          },
          {
            type: 'button',
            text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Approve & resume', emoji: true },
            value: state.sessionId,
            action_id: 'approve_agent_action',
          },
          {
            type: 'button',
            text: { type: 'plain_text', text: 'Reject & abort', emoji: true },
            style: 'danger',
            value: state.sessionId,
            action_id: 'reject_agent_action',
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
  });
}

Slack expects an acknowledgment of an interaction within three seconds, so Bolt’s ack() is called first and the slower work follows. Claiming must be atomic, otherwise two AEs can click at once and both believe they own the deal.
// slackHandlers.ts
import { App } from '@slack/bolt';

const app = new App({
  token: process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN,
  signingSecret: process.env.SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET, // Bolt verifies request signatures
});

app.action('claim_deal_session', async ({ ack, body, client }) => {
  await ack();
  const action = (body as any).actions[0];
  const sessionId: string = action.value;
  const userId = body.user.id;

  // Atomic claim: only the first caller succeeds (e.g. a conditional write in your database).
  const claimed = await tryClaimSession(sessionId, userId);
  if (!claimed) {
    await client.chat.postEphemeral({
      channel: (body as any).channel.id,
      user: userId,
      text: 'Someone else has already claimed this session.',
    });
    return;
  }

  await client.chat.update({
    channel: (body as any).channel.id,
    ts: (body as any).message.ts,
    text: `Session ${sessionId} claimed by <@${userId}>`,
    blocks: [
      {
        type: 'section',
        text: { type: 'mrkdwn', text: `*Session claimed by <@${userId}>.* Human control is active.` },
      },
      {
        type: 'context',
        elements: [{ type: 'mrkdwn', text: `Session ID: \`${sessionId}\`` }],
      },
    ],
  });

  // Give the AE access through your own authenticated, expiring session viewer.
  const url = await createHandoffUrl(sessionId, userId);
  await client.chat.postEphemeral({
    channel: (body as any).channel.id,
    user: userId,
    text: `You now control session \`${sessionId}\`: ${url}`,
  });
});

app.action('approve_agent_action', async ({ ack, body }) => {
  await ack();
  const sessionId: string = (body as any).actions[0].value;
  await recordDecision(sessionId, body.user.id, 'approved'); // audit log
  await resumeAgentSession(sessionId);
});

app.action('reject_agent_action', async ({ ack, body }) => {
  await ack();
  const sessionId: string = (body as any).actions[0].value;
  await recordDecision(sessionId, body.user.id, 'rejected');
  await abortAgentSession(sessionId);
});

// --- Placeholders for your own infrastructure ---
async function tryClaimSession(sessionId: string, userId: string): Promise<boolean> { return true; }
async function createHandoffUrl(sessionId: string, userId: string): Promise<string> {
  // Return a short-lived, authenticated link to your remote-browser viewer
  // (for example one built on a WebRTC or VNC-based stack). Do not embed
  // the user ID as the credential.
  return `https://warroom.example.com/session/${sessionId}`;
}
async function recordDecision(sessionId: string, userId: string, decision: string) {}
async function resumeAgentSession(sessionId: string) {}
async function abortAgentSession(sessionId: string) {}

(async () => { await app.start(process.env.PORT || 3000); })();

Design notes:
Restrict who can approve. Check the clicking user against your approval matrix (role, region, deal-size authority) before resuming. Slack channel membership alone is not authorization.
Treat the handoff link as a credential. It should be authenticated, short-lived and single-use.
Keep an audit trail. Log every pause, claim, approval and rejection with a timestamp and user ID, and write a summary to your CRM (for example Salesforce or HubSpot) through its API.
The thresholds below are illustrative. Set them from your own approval matrix.



Trigger
Agent action
Interception condition
War room protocol




Large deal value
Fills billing data, selects a standard tier
Proposed ACV at or above your threshold (for example $25,000)

Claim: The account’s AE, or a round-robin AE, claims the card and decides whether to approve, change terms or take over.


Non-standard legal terms
Detects an unrecognized clause or terms field
Any unrecognized legal checkbox or free-text contract field

Legal routing: Card goes to a shared legal and revenue-operations channel.


Seat or tier limit exceeded
Reaches the maximum seat allocation
Seats requested above your policy limit

Review: AE reviews and may offer a custom package.


Login, CAPTCHA or bot challenge
Encounters a verification step
Agent signals it cannot proceed, or a challenge page is detected

Human handoff: A person completes the step through a remote view and hands back. Do not engineer ways around such challenges.


Payment or signature step
Reaches a payment, contract acceptance or e-signature screen
Always

Mandatory approval: Never let an agent complete these without human sign-off.



Potential benefits
Faster handling of routine work. Agents can take on data gathering, form completion and research, so people focus on negotiation and approvals. The share of work an agent can absorb varies widely by workflow, so measure it in your own pilot rather than assuming a fixed figure.
Faster escalation. A Slack notification reaches the right person in seconds, but how quickly the deal moves still depends on whether someone responds. Set response-time targets and an escalation path, such as re-pinging after a few minutes and falling back to a backup AE.
Auditability and governance. Logged pauses, claims and overrides, with screenshots, give compliance and sales leadership a record of who approved what.
Less manual entry for AEs. Reps spend less time on procurement paperwork and more on relationship work.
Limits and risks
Agents still make mistakes. OpenAI describes its agent products as capable but fallible, and says it designs them to ask for confirmation before consequential actions. That is why the approval gate belongs in your control plane and not only in the vendor’s defaults.
Prompt injection from web pages is a real risk for browsing agents. Do not give an agent credentials or tool access beyond what the task needs, and keep payment and signature actions behind human approval.
Vendor products change quickly. Operator was retired as a standalone product, Windsurf was rebranded, and Manus changed ownership twice within a year. Keep the policy and Slack layers independent of any one agent so you can swap the runtime.
Review data-handling terms for customer data, screenshots and transcripts before sending them to Slack or a third-party agent.
Autonomous agents such as ChatGPT agent (the successor to Operator), Devin and Manus can shorten the repetitive parts of the revenue cycle, but enterprise deals need human judgment at the points where money, legal terms or customer relationships are on the line. A control plane that pauses the agent, packages the context and routes it to a Slack war room lets a person step in at those moments and then hand work back to the agent.
Decide where you can intercept: your own agent loop, vendor webhooks, or permission scoping for hosted agents.
Define an approval matrix covering deal size, discount limits, legal terms, regions and who may approve each.
Build the policy engine, session store and audit log first, then the Slack layer.
Create the Slack app with Block Kit messages and interactivity, with signature verification and atomic claiming.
Pilot on a low-risk workflow, track pause rate, time to claim and override rate, and tighten the policies from the data.
Train AEs on claiming sessions and taking over via the handoff viewer.
